qa(None, "How did you design the ablation study, and what is its main pitfall?",
   f"I took the full set D (phase, hormones, PSST, BDI, BAI), dropped one group at a time, refitted the whole nested-CV pipeline and compared the out-of-sample RMSE with the full model, using the same resampled women (change in RMSE with a 95% interval). I also fitted each group alone. The pitfall is shared credit: if two groups carry overlapping information (BDI and BAI), dropping one costs little because the other covers for it, while alone it looks strong. So drop-one answers 'what does this group add to the others?', and group-alone answers 'how much can it do by itself?'; both are shown (pooled, {npw}).")
qa(None, "What did the ablation show about hormones, phase and the questionnaires?",
   f"Hormones add nothing: DRSP R2 (pooled) was {ci(E5M, dict(sample='pooled', target='drsp_psych', model='ridge', fset='D-hormones'), 'R2_oos', '{:+.3f}', label='E5 drop hormones pooled drsp')} without them versus {ci('results/phase4_metrics.csv', dict(sample='pooled', target='drsp_psych', model='ridge', fset='D'), 'R2_oos', '{:+.3f}', label='E5 full D pooled drsp')} with them, and hormones alone gave {ci(E5M, dict(sample='pooled', target='drsp_psych', model='ridge', fset='only-hormones'), 'R2_oos', '{:+.3f}', label='E5 only hormones pooled drsp')}. Phase matters a little for DRSP only (dropping it raised RMSE by {ci(E5D, dict(sample='pooled', target='drsp_psych', first='ridge/D-phase', second='ridge/D', metric='RMSE'), 'delta', '{:+.3f}', lo='lo', hi='hi', label='E5 dRMSE drop phase pooled drsp')}). BDI is the most valuable single group (R2 without it {ci(E5M, dict(sample='pooled', target='drsp_psych', model='ridge', fset='D-BDI'), 'R2_oos', '{:+.3f}', label='E5 drop BDI pooled drsp')}), and the three questionnaires together reproduce almost all of set D ({ci(E5M, dict(sample='pooled', target='drsp_psych', model='ridge', fset='only-questionnaires'), 'R2_oos', '{:+.3f}', label='E5 only questionnaires pooled drsp')}).")
qa(None, "What is a learning curve and what does yours show?",
   f"A learning curve shows test (and training) performance as the number of training women grows. For the questionnaire model (set D, DRSP, pooled, {npw}) the test R2 was {ci(E5L, dict(sample='pooled', target='drsp_psych', fset='D', n_train_women=K1), 'R2_oos', '{:+.2f}', label='E5 LC D pooled drsp k=10')} with {kc(K1)} training women (training R2 {val(E5L, dict(sample='pooled', target='drsp_psych', fset='D', n_train_women=K1), 'train_R2', '{:.2f}', label='E5 LC train D pooled drsp k=10')}: heavy overfitting), {ci(E5L, dict(sample='pooled', target='drsp_psych', fset='D', n_train_women=K2), 'R2_oos', '{:+.2f}', label='E5 LC D pooled drsp k=20')} with {kc(K2)}, and {ci(E5L, dict(sample='pooled', target='drsp_psych', fset='D', n_train_women=kmax('pooled')), 'R2_oos', '{:+.2f}', label='E5 LC D pooled drsp kmax')} with all other women, with the training R2 falling towards it ({val(E5L, dict(sample='pooled', target='drsp_psych', fset='D', n_train_women=kmax('pooled')), 'train_R2', '{:.2f}', label='E5 LC train D pooled drsp kmax')}). The curve is flat from about {kc(K2)} women on.")
qa(None, "Would more women help? What can you and what can you not conclude from the learning curves?",
   f"For this ridge model and these predictors the test curve is flat over the range I could examine, so more women of the same kind would probably add little accuracy: the limit is the information in the predictors, not the sample size. For phase plus hormones (set C) the curve stays near zero (DRSP, pooled, all women: {val(E5L, dict(sample='pooled', target='drsp_psych', fset='C', n_train_women=kmax('pooled')), 'R2_oos', '{:+.3f}', label='E5 LC C pooled drsp kmax')}). What I cannot conclude: (i) that a much larger study, or more cycles per woman, or better hormone assays could not reveal an effect: I cannot extrapolate beyond {calc('E5 k max pooled again', kmax('pooled'), '{:.0f}', E5L, 'largest k')} training women; (ii) that other model types would plateau at the same level; (iii) anything finer than the wide confidence intervals, since each point uses one random draw of the training women.")
